KZ3 patch 1150 (SPU pass 139): a SPURS task dispatched by the taskset exit scan or by spu_taskset_dispatch runs as the task id its
dispatcher chose, passed as an argument (spu_dispatch_async_ids) instead of through the globals g_ydkj_real_taskset_ea /
g_ydkj_real_taskid. Credit: the Killzone 2 port, its patch 7004 (same race, found there first).
Why: cellSpursCreateTask writes those globals outside the taskset scan's lock on its PPU thread; landing between the scan's write
(spu_taskset_task_exited / spu_taskset_dispatch) and spu_workload_dispatch_async's read, it gave the job another task's id: the job ran
its image with that task's TaskInfo (argument, context), its exit cleared that task's bits, and its own task stayed enabled and
running with nothing behind it. spu_async_run's image-22 (YDKJ cri) paths now build their context from the job's ids too. The
workload-policy and sys_event callers of spu_workload_dispatch_async keep the globals (the last taskset and task the PPU created, what
they were written for). spu_async_run logs "[taskset] MISMATCH" (first 16, then every power of two) when a job's image is not the ELF
its task's TaskInfo names (TaskInfo at taskset + 0x80 + 48 * task, the ELF EA's low word at +0x14, flags masked): a scheduler fault.
SPU_TASK_IDS_GLOBAL=1 restores the old hand-over (A/B only). Host test tools/spu/test_r139_task_ids.sh.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.19.0 (group B pass 2, R&C 2026-11-09, on KZ3's group A r152): builds on #515 (ps3_env_once): spu_async_run's
YDKJ_MINIMAL_CTX test is `j->taskset_ea && !ps3_env_once("YDKJ_MINIMAL_CTX")`. Upstream's new start path
(spu_taskset_line_committed -> spu_taskset_start_pending -> spu_taskset_task_exited) dispatches through
spu_dispatch_async_ids too.

